        {
          "post_id": "533670",
          "text": "<p>Assume that $\\texttt{char}$ is $8$ bits and signed integers are represented using $2$'s complement. Consider the following C code:</p><pre data-rawcode=\"#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;  int main() {     signed char x = 0xF9;     unsigned char y = x;      printf(&quot;%d %u&quot;, x, (unsigned int)y);     return 0; }\" data-pbcklang=\"c_cpp\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" data-linenums=\"true\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-c_cpp\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    signed char x = 0xF9;\n    unsigned char y = x;\n\n    printf(\"%d %u\", x, (unsigned int)y);\n    return 0;\n}</pre><p data-start=\"369\" data-end=\"393\">What will be the output?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"487\">$\\texttt{-7\\ 249}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"487\">$\\texttt{249\\ 249}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"487\">$\\texttt{-7\\ -7}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"487\">$\\texttt{7\\ 249}$</li></ol>",
